> Byte semantics and metric scope

The API compares complete std::string byte sequences, including embedded NUL. It
does not decode Unicode code points, grapheme clusters or canonically equivalent
spellings. UTF-8 e-acute consists of two bytes, so comparing those two bytes
with ASCII e can cost2. Adjacent transposition is not a single Levenshtein edit.
The resulting unit byte distance is symmetric and obeys the triangle inequality:
reverse an edit path for symmetry and concatenate paths for the triangle bound.
These statements concern this fixed edit alphabet and costs, not visually
perceived text similarity.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/levenshtein_bytes.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::string>> pairs{
        {std::string("a\0b", 3), std::string("a\0c", 3)}, {"\xc3\xa9", "e"},
        {"ab", "ba"}, {"", "abc"}
    };
    std::vector<std::string> labels{"embedded NUL", "UTF8 bytes",
                                    "transposition", "empty prefix"};
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| explicit byte control | actual first byte length | actual second byte length | actual " "distance | actual reverse distance |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < pairs.size(); ++i) {
        auto & [a, b] = pairs[i];
        auto d = dm::algorithms::levenshtein_distance(a, b);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", labels[i], a.size(),
                   b.size(), d, dm::algorithms::levenshtein_distance(b, a));
        x.push_back(i);
        y.push_back(d);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "actual byte distance", {37, 99, 235}, 6).y_limits(-.2, 4.5).title("Actual byte semantics on explicit controls").x_label("listed control index").y_label("minimum unit byte edits").save_svg("build/levenshtein_bytes.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| explicit byte control | actual first byte length | actual second byte length | actual distance | actual reverse distance |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| embedded NUL | 3 | 3 | 1 | 1 |
| UTF8 bytes | 2 | 1 | 2 | 2 |
| transposition | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 |
| empty prefix | 0 | 3 | 3 | 3 |
\image(figures/levenshtein_bytes.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual embedded-NUL, UTF-8, transposition and empty controls.)
\alttext(The table identifies every raw-byte experiment without displaying binary NUL content; no Unicode or transposition-aware semantics are implied.)

> Rolling storage versus full capture

For n=m, the ordinary/count-only producer evaluates n^2 cells but keeps
only2*(n+1) rolling slots. This storage formula is mathematical geometry, not an
allocator or RAM measurement. Optional full capture retains(n+1)^2 actual table
cells plus inputs and optimal path, so it intentionally gives up the rolling
memory advantage. The following actual count-only runs show producing cell
counts and equal comparisons; equalities count all DP cell byte matches, not
aligned matches or edit-script length.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/levenshtein_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, cells, slots;
    std::print("| original bytes per side | actual cell comparisons | actual equal comparisons | " "theoretical rolling slots, not measured memory |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int n : {8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256}) {
        std::string a(n, 'a'), b(n, 'b');
        auto r = dm::algorithms::levenshtein_analyze(a, b);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, r.work.cells,
                   r.work.equalities, 2 * (n + 1));
        x.push_back(n);
        cells.push_back(r.work.cells);
        slots.push_back(2 * (n + 1));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), q = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, cells, "actual cells", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, cells, "",
                                                              {37, 99, 235}, 4).title("Actual producing work").x_label("bytes per side").y_label("actual cell comparisons");
    q.line(x, slots, "theoretical rolling slots", {22, 163, 74}, 2).title(
        "Explicit runtime storage geometry").x_label("bytes per side").y_label(
        "two-row slots, not RAM");
    dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 1).add(p).add(q).size(900, 800).title(
        "Actual work and separately labelled storage formula").fit_panels().save_svg("build/levenshtein_work.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| original bytes per side | actual cell comparisons | actual equal comparisons | theoretical rolling slots, not measured memory |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 8 | 64 | 0 | 18 |
| 16 | 256 | 0 | 34 |
| 32 | 1024 | 0 | 66 |
| 64 | 4096 | 0 | 130 |
| 128 | 16384 | 0 | 258 |
| 256 | 65536 | 0 | 514 |
\image(figures/levenshtein_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual cell work beside the explicitly theoretical rolling-storage formula.)
\alttext(Separate panels distinguish measured producer counts from logical storage geometry; optional full-table capture is not called linear space.)

> Contracts and verification

Empty inputs are valid. Complete row/cell/distance-increment geometry is checked
before allocation. Scalar/count-only calls retain two rows and perform no
traceback. Capture conservatively admits all input bytes, every table cell and
up to n+m move records before copying/evaluation. captured_entries reports
actual input/table/path records, so conservative admission can reject an
eventual shorter path. The default million-record cap excludes
fields/capacities/runtime rows/output copies and bytes/RAM;0 disables only the
optional cap. All retained strings, rows and moves are owned.

An independent full-table byte reference verified500 original-orientation pairs
against actual rolling-row snapshots and distances. Another1000 arbitrary-byte
scalar results matched the frozen implementation exactly. Native tests
independently traverse all short binary edit graphs, verify native optimal path
costs and retain every original pair plus
NUL/UTF8/transposition/empty/orientation/quota/ownership controls. The complete
ten-report batch passed77 native CTest cases,39 directly instrumented ASan/UBSan
cases,106 compiled/executed public examples,479/479 scoped definitions and ten
representative clangd hovers. All4 complete report programs executed and all4
native figures were visually reviewed. HTML/Markdown computed result and
identical source/build/export figure bytes passed verification. Repeatable
receipts are retained under `build/mepml-conversion/batch-194-203/`.

> References

R. A. Wagner and M. J. Fischer(1974),
[The String-to-String Correction Problem|https://doi.org/10.1145/321796.321811],
Journal of the ACM21(1),168–173. The implementation specializes the recurrence
to unit byte insertion/deletion/substitution costs and uses rolling rows for
ordinary calls.
